 @notice FIPS 180-4 SHA-512 over a memory byte string.
 @dev The EVM exposes SHA-256 as a precompile but not SHA-512, and ed25519 is defined over
      SHA-512: the challenge scalar is `SHA512(R || A || M)`. Verifying a Solana owner's signature
      on EVM/Tron therefore needs the compression function in software.
      The compression loop is Yul over full 256-bit words, with arithmetic taken modulo 2^64 by
      masking (see the note in `hash`). The 80 round constants live in one packed `bytes`
      constant and are read back eight bytes at a time, which keeps the deployed code far smaller
      than 80 immediates would.

@notice EVM equivalent of `create_program_address`: hash a fixed bump into the seeds.
 @param seeds The seed bytes concatenated (Solana hashes seeds back-to-back, no separators).
 @param bump  The bump seed byte.
 @param programId The owning program id (raw 32 bytes).
 @return ok  False when the derived address is on-curve (an invalid PDA).
 @return pda The derived address.

@notice EVM equivalent of `find_program_address`: walk bumps 255->0, return the first
         off-curve (canonical) address. Reverts if none exists (cryptographically impossible).
 @dev The preimage `seeds||bump||programId||marker` is built once; each iteration only rewrites
      the single bump byte in place and re-hashes via the sha256 precompile, avoiding a fresh
      `abi.encodePacked` allocation+copy per step.

@dev Clears the active inbox and every route-asset marking. Transient backends must also drop
 the markings: they only self-clear at the end of the transaction, and a later route in the same
 transaction (possibly on the same inbox) must start with an empty asset set.

@notice Seeds the route context for the current execution.
 @dev Records the active inbox and current-chain ERC-20 asset tokens so inbox callbacks are
 scoped to this route. Native-token entries are skipped and guarded by the route balance invariant.
 At most one route scope is open at a time, so a nested begin reverts instead of reusing it.

@title RouteScopeBase
 @author Agustin Aguilar
 @notice Chain-independent route-scope state: the active inbox and the set of route-accounted
 assets that inbox callbacks are allowed to touch.
 @dev The backing store is supplied by a chain-specific implementation of the primitives below:
 ordinary storage on Shanghai targets (Tron, see RouteScopeStorage) and EIP-1153 transient
 storage on Cancun targets (EVM, see TrailsPdasEvm). The seeding helpers and the authorization
 policy that consumes this state live alongside the registry in RouteAdapters.

@title RouteScopeStorage
 @author Agustin Aguilar
 @notice Ordinary-storage scope backend for Shanghai targets (Tron) that lack EIP-1153 transient
 storage. The scope is held in regular storage and explicitly cleared after each route; an
 auxiliary token list is kept solely so the clear can enumerate the marked assets.
 @dev The primitives are left `virtual` so the EVM deployment can override them with a transient
 backend (see `evm/TrailsPdasEvm.sol`). When overridden, the storage slots declared here are
 simply never written, which carries no runtime cost.

